<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Which source this build came from.

    The version says 1.2.3; it does not say which commit that was cut from, and a person
    holding a binary and reading the repository has no way to line the two up. The SDK
    appends "+<SourceRevisionId>" to the informational version, so setting it puts the
    commit inside the assembly, where `cairn diagnostics` reports it — matching the commit
    the release manifest and the build attestation both name.

    Read from GITHUB_SHA rather than by shelling out to git: a build from a tarball, or one
    in a container with no .git, would otherwise stamp nothing or fail. Empty off CI, which
    is right — a local build is not from a commit in any useful sense, and one that claims
    to be would be believed.

    ContinuousIntegrationBuild normalises the source paths embedded by the compiler, so two
    builds of the same commit produce the same managed assemblies instead of differing by
    whatever directory the runner happened to use. It does not make the published artifact
    byte-for-byte reproducible — the single-file bundle and, on macOS, a signature carrying
    a timestamp and a stapled notarisation ticket, all see to that — but it is what lets
    somebody rebuild a commit and compare the assemblies inside.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    <SourceRevisionId Condition="'$(SourceRevisionId)' == ''">$(GITHUB_SHA)</SourceRevisionId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Carried into the assembly so the terms travel with the binary. Cairn is source-available
    under the PolyForm Strict License 1.0.0 — see LICENSE.md, which is the document that
    governs; this is metadata, not a grant.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Company>Dave (Dizzy) Smith</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright 2026 Dave (Dizzy) Smith. PolyForm Strict License 1.0.0.</Copyright>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What actually shipped.

    Every PackageReference here is a minimum-version range, so the graph is decided at
    restore time rather than by this repository — 33 packages resolved, including native
    payloads that go inside the signed artifact (SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.*,
    HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.*, Avalonia.Angle.Windows.Natives, Tmds.DBus.Protocol) that
    no .csproj names. Without a lock file there is no record of which versions a given
    release was built from, so a compromise is neither detectable afterwards nor
    reproducible.

    Locked mode only under CI, which GitHub Actions signals with CI=true. Updating a package
    locally should rewrite the lock file as a matter of course; a release build should fail
    rather than quietly resolve something the lock does not name.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every runtime a lock file has to answer for.

    A lock file records the graph per runtime identifier, and restore in locked mode fails
    if the one it needs is not in there. Nothing declared these, so the lock held whatever
    RID the machine that wrote it happened to use — osx-arm64, from a Mac — and the first
    Linux runner to restore it was refused: "the project's runtime identifiers have changed
    from. Project's runtime identifiers: linux-x64, lock file's runtime identifiers
    osx-arm64." It would have failed for win-x64 and osx-x64 in the release build too.

    The same four build-release.sh publishes, so the lock covers a release rather than
    covering whichever quarter of one was cut last. Declared for every project and not only
    the three that are published: a RID-specific restore of the launcher restores what it
    references the same way, so Cairn.Core needs the RIDs in its lock as much as the
    launcher does, and deciding case by case which project is in that graph is how this
    happened in the first place.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>osx-arm64;osx-x64;win-x64;linux-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NuGetAudit is on by default in the .NET 10 SDK, but it reports NU1901-NU1904 as
    warnings — and the release publish ran with `-v quiet >/dev/null`, so a known-vulnerable
    transitive was a warning written to a discarded log in the job that ships. Promoted to
    errors so it stops something instead.

    `all` rather than the default `direct`, because the packages most likely to carry an
    advisory here are exactly the ones nothing names: the native asset packages arrive
    transitively through Avalonia.

    The cost is real and deliberate: a newly published advisory against anything in the
    graph breaks the build until it is dealt with, including locally. That is the point —
    the alternative is finding out from the log of a release that already happened.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
